## 1. All instances in both MIGs (version check)

The command needs the MIG name and region. Names and region are from the hostnames, so confirm them in the output.

```bash
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list --format="table(name,region,targetSize)"
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances cn-backend-mig-regional --region=me-central2 --format="table(name,status,instanceStatus,version.instanceTemplate.basename())"
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances cn-frontend-mig-regional --region=me-central2 --format="table(name,status,instanceStatus,version.instanceTemplate.basename())"
```

All instances should show the same template. If two templates appear, a rollout is incomplete.

## 2. What sits behind 136.68.140.144 (LB side)

```bash
gcloud compute addresses list --filter="address=136.68.140.144"
gcloud compute forwarding-rules list --filter="IPAddress=136.68.140.144" --format="yaml(name,target,loadBalancingScheme,portRange,region)"
gcloud compute url-maps list
gcloud compute backend-services list --format="table(name,backends[].group.basename(),securityPolicy.basename(),region)"
```

Then, for the url-map and backend services found above, show the host and path routing:
```bash
gcloud compute url-maps describe <URL_MAP_NAME> --format="yaml(hostRules,pathMatchers,defaultService)"   # substitute the name from url-maps list
```

This shows which backends serve `stagingapi`. It also shows whether other services, such as the AI test VM from your earlier path-routing setup, sit behind the same IP.

